{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And Canaan begot Sidon his firstborn.</b> Here Scripture enumerates ten who came from the seed of Canaan, all of whom were given to Abraham our father in the gift of the land — this is what was said to him in the covenant between the pieces (Genesis 15:18–19): ״לזרעך נתתי את הארץ הזאת מנהר מצרים עד הנהר הגדול נהר פרת, את הקיני ואת הקנזי ואת הקדמוני״ — and it goes on enumerating ten nations. And the reason their names changed is that in this passage they are the names their father called them at their birth, whereas in the gift of the land their names changed according to the change of tongues and lands, for when they separated into their lands, to their nations, they were called by other names. And if you count them you will find eleven, but Sidon his firstborn is the one called Canaanite; he is joined with one of his brothers who did not become a nation." "<b>And know</b> that of these ten nations, seven of them Israel conquered in the days of Joshua, but the three — the Kenite, the Kenizzite, and the Kadmonite — they never conquered. If so, the passage spoken to Abraham in the gift of the land, in which these nations are mentioned, alludes to the time to come, when they will be subjugated to us. And behold, the thoughts of the Supreme Being are deep, for He wanted the Holy Land to belong to Canaan — he is the slave who was enslaved to the sons of Shem by the word of his father — and it was delivered into his hand from the time of the Dispersion, so that he would not be able to establish a claim of possession, for a slave cannot establish a claim of possession in his master's property. For had it been delivered into the hand of another nation, perhaps they would have established a claim in it — but not so with a slave."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:38.696176205Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}